-- Q9 [Prokaryotic Genetics: Transduction · medium · Skill 2 · ans A] (g84)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Prokaryotic Genetics: Transduction',
    $qt84$A virulent (lytic) bacteriophage infecting an antibiotic-resistant Bacillus culture occasionally errs during phage assembly, packaging a fragment of the donor cell's chromosome carrying the resistance allele into a new virion instead of viral DNA. That virion later injects the bacterial fragment into a susceptible recipient cell, which then becomes antibiotic-resistant. The mechanism of gene transfer described is most accurately identified as:$qt84$,
    $op84$[{"label":"A","text":"transduction, because a bacteriophage acted as the vector that carried the donor's chromosomal DNA into the recipient cell"},{"label":"B","text":"transformation, because the recipient took up naked donor DNA released into the surrounding environment"},{"label":"C","text":"conjugation, because donor DNA passed to the recipient through a pilus that drew the two cells into direct contact"},{"label":"D","text":"transposition, because a mobile genetic element excised itself and inserted into the recipient's chromosome"}]$op84$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex84$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is A because the donor's antibiotic-resistance gene is moved to the recipient cell inside a bacteriophage particle, which is the defining feature of transduction. During the lytic cycle, phage assembly machinery "occasionally makes a mistake. Instead of packaging viral DNA, it takes a random piece of host DNA and inserts it into the capsid. Once released, this virion will then inject the former host's DNA into a newly infected host" (microbiology Ch.6, ~line 10280). Transduction is uniquely the horizontal-gene-transfer route that "genes are transferred between cells in a virus" (Ch.11, ~line 19373); the accidental packaging of a random chromosomal fragment during the lytic cycle described here is generalized transduction. (Choice A) Correct: a bacteriophage is the vector, matching "Transduction occurs when a bacteriophage transfers bacterial DNA from one bacterium to another during sequential infections" (~line 10277). (Choice B) Transformation is "naked DNA is taken up from the environment" (~line 19372); no naked, cell-free DNA is involved here, since the resistance gene travels enclosed in a phage capsid, so this is wrong. (Choice C) Conjugation requires "a hollow tube called a conjugation pilus to transfer genes between cells" (~line 19374) in direct cell-to-cell contact; no pilus or donor-recipient bridge is present, so this is wrong. (Choice D) Transposition involves a mobile element that excises and reinserts within or between DNA molecules; it does not by itself carry a gene from one separate cell to another inside a viral particle, so it does not describe this inter-cell transfer. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must map the specific situation of phage-mediated DNA movement onto the correct horizontal-gene-transfer mechanism and rule out the other named pathways.$ex84$,
    'medium', '2B', $cc84$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc84$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a84B$Names a real HGT mechanism (transformation) but requires environmental naked DNA rather than a viral vector$a84B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a84C$Names a real HGT mechanism (conjugation) requiring a pilus/direct contact, absent from the scenario$a84C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a84D$Confuses an intragenomic mobile element (transposon) with an inter-cellular transfer mechanism$a84D$ FROM q;

-- Q10 [Prokaryotic Genetics: Transduction · hard · Skill 2 · ans B] (g85)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Prokaryotic Genetics: Transduction',
    $qt85$A researcher mixes a streptomycin-resistant \(E.\ coli\) donor strain with a streptomycin-sensitive recipient strain in a chamber whose two halves are separated by a fine filter that permits fluids but blocks cell-to-cell contact, and the medium is supplemented with DNase that destroys any free DNA. Resistant recipient colonies still arise, and resistance fails to appear when an inhibitor inactivates a co-cultured bacteriophage. Which mechanism of horizontal gene transfer is responsible?$qt85$,
    $op85$[{"label":"A","text":"Transformation, because the recipient takes up the resistance gene as naked DNA released into the shared medium"},{"label":"B","text":"Transduction, because the resistance gene is carried into the recipient packaged inside a bacteriophage"},{"label":"C","text":"Conjugation, because the resistance gene passes from donor to recipient through a conjugation pilus and cytoplasmic bridge"},{"label":"D","text":"Transposition, because the resistance gene is a mobile element that jumps directly from the donor genome into the recipient genome"}]$op85$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex85$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is B because transduction is the only horizontal gene transfer mechanism in which bacterial DNA is moved between cells protected inside a bacteriophage, which is exactly what the two experimental clues isolate. The textbook lists three prokaryotic mechanisms: "Transformation: naked DNA is taken up from the environment," "Transduction: genes are transferred between cells in a virus," and "Conjugation: use of a hollow tube called a conjugation pilus to transfer genes between cells" (Microbiology, lines 19372-19374). The DNase in the medium destroys any free DNA, so transformation, which depends on naked environmental DNA, cannot occur, while the filter blocks the cell-to-cell contact that conjugation requires. Because resistance still appears and disappears only when the bacteriophage is inactivated, the gene must travel inside a virus: "Viruses that infect bacteria (bacteriophages) may also move short pieces of chromosomal DNA from one bacterium to another in a process called transduction" (Microbiology, lines 19433-19434). (Choice A) Transformation is ruled out: it requires the recipient to take up "naked DNA found in its environment," but the Avery-MacLeod-McCarty work showed that when DNA "was degraded, the resulting mixture was no longer able to transform the R strain bacteria" (Microbiology, lines 19400, 16765-16766), so DNase abolishes this route. (Choice B) (Choice C) Conjugation is the tempting partial truth: it does transfer resistance genes and is unaffected by DNase because the DNA travels through a protected bridge, but it requires that DNA move "through a cytoplasmic bridge after a conjugation pilus draws the two cells close enough to form the" bridge (Microbiology, line 19382); the filter blocking cell-to-cell contact makes conjugation impossible. (Choice D) Transposition does not transfer genes between cells at all; transposons "are mobile segments of DNA that can move within the genome of an organism," an intracellular event that cannot account for resistance crossing from donor to recipient. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must combine the DNase result, the contact-blocking filter, and the phage-dependence result to eliminate competing mechanisms and identify the one transfer route that requires a viral vector.$ex85$,
    'hard', '2B', $cc85$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc85$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
