-- Q16 [Viral Genomes and Replication Strategy · hard · Skill 2 · ans B] (g93)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Viral Genomes and Replication Strategy',
    $qt93$Three viruses simultaneously infect and uncoat inside the same host cell: a (+)sense single-stranded RNA picornavirus, a (−)sense single-stranded RNA virus, and a retrovirus. For each genome, the FIRST enzyme-driven molecular event immediately after uncoating is most likely:$qt93$,
    $op93$[{"label":"A","text":"picornavirus genome is copied to a complementary strand by a viral polymerase; (−)sense genome is translated directly by host ribosomes; retrovirus genome is reverse-transcribed into DNA"},{"label":"B","text":"picornavirus genome is translated directly by host ribosomes; (−)sense genome is copied to a (+)sense strand by a virion-carried RNA-dependent RNA polymerase; retrovirus genome is reverse-transcribed into DNA by a virion-carried reverse transcriptase"},{"label":"C","text":"picornavirus genome is reverse-transcribed into DNA by reverse transcriptase; (−)sense genome is translated directly by host ribosomes; retrovirus genome is copied to a (+)sense strand by an RNA-dependent RNA polymerase"},{"label":"D","text":"picornavirus genome is translated directly by host ribosomes; (−)sense genome is reverse-transcribed into DNA by a virion-carried reverse transcriptase; retrovirus genome is copied to a (+)sense strand by an RNA-dependent RNA polymerase"}]$op93$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex93$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is B because a (+)sense RNA genome functions as mRNA and is translated immediately, a (−)sense RNA genome must first be copied to a (+)sense strand by a virion-carried RNA-dependent RNA polymerase before any protein can be made, and a retrovirus (despite being (+)sense) does not translate first but instead reverse-transcribes its RNA into DNA using a virion-carried reverse transcriptase. The text states that "If a virus has a +ssRNA genome, it can be translated directly to make viral proteins. Viral genomic +ssRNA acts like cellular mRNA," whereas "if a virus contains a −ssRNA genome, the host ribosomes cannot translate it until the −ssRNA is replicated into +ssRNA by viral RNA-dependent RNA polymerase (RdRP)... The RdRP is brought in by the virus." For the retrovirus, the text specifies that "Single-stranded RNA viruses such as HIV carry a special enzyme called reverse transcriptase within the capsid that synthesizes a complementary ssDNA (cDNA) copy using the +ssRNA genome as a template." The polarity trap is that a retrovirus is itself (+)sense, so a test-taker who reasons only from polarity would predict immediate translation; the distinguishing feature is the virion-packaged enzyme that diverts the genome to DNA first. (Choice A) reverses the picornavirus and (−)sense behaviors, having the readable (+)sense genome copied and the unreadable (−)sense genome translated. (Choice B) is correct: each genome's first event matches its required machinery — direct translation, copy-then-translate, and reverse-transcribe-then-integrate. (Choice C) wrongly assigns reverse transcription to the picornavirus and demotes the retrovirus to mere RNA-to-RNA copying, ignoring its packaged reverse transcriptase. (Choice D) is the partial-truth trap: it correctly translates the (+)sense picornavirus but swaps the (−)sense and retrovirus events, attributing reverse transcriptase to the (−)sense virus (which actually carries RdRP) and RdRP-style copying to the retrovirus. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between genome polarity and required viral enzymes to predict, for three distinct genomes, which molecular event must occur first after uncoating.$ex93$,
    'hard', '2B', $cc93$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc93$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a93A$Swaps the readable (+)sense and unreadable (−)sense behaviors so the (+)sense genome is copied and the (−)sense genome is translated$a93A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a93C$Assigns reverse transcription to the wrong virus and reduces the retrovirus to simple RNA-templated RNA copying, ignoring its packaged reverse transcriptase$a93C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a93D$Gets the (+)sense picornavirus right but interchanges the enzymes of the (−)sense virus (RdRP) and the retrovirus (reverse transcriptase)$a93D$ FROM q;

-- Q17 [Viral Genomes and Replication Strategy · hard · Skill 2 · ans C] (g94)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Viral Genomes and Replication Strategy',
    $qt94$A nucleoside-analog drug is given to a patient infected with an HIV-like enveloped \(+\)ssRNA retrovirus. The drug arrests the virus's life cycle by blocking the conversion of the incoming RNA genome into the double-stranded DNA that integrates into the host chromosome, yet the patient's own chromosomal DNA continues to replicate normally during cell division. The viral enzyme the drug most likely inhibits, and the reason this step is uniquely vulnerable, is BEST identified as which of the following?$qt94$,
    $op94$[{"label":"A","text":"Host DNA-dependent DNA polymerase, because the retrovirus hijacks the cell's own replication enzyme to copy its genome, making that polymerase the only available target."},{"label":"B","text":"A viral DNA-dependent DNA polymerase, because retroviruses package their own DNA polymerase to copy a DNA template, an enzyme the host cell does not possess."},{"label":"C","text":"Reverse transcriptase, an RNA-dependent DNA polymerase, because synthesizing DNA from an RNA template is a reaction the host cell never performs, so inhibiting it spares the host's own DNA replication."},{"label":"D","text":"Viral integrase, because it carries out the RNA-to-DNA conversion before splicing the product into the host chromosome, a recombination reaction the host cannot perform."}]$op94$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex94$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is C because the enzyme that copies a retrovirus's RNA genome into DNA is reverse transcriptase, an RNA-dependent DNA polymerase that has no counterpart in the host cell, so blocking it stops the virus without touching host DNA replication. The text states that retroviruses "carry a special enzyme called reverse transcriptase within the capsid that synthesizes a complementary ssDNA (cDNA) copy using the +ssRNA genome as a template," after which "the ssDNA is then made into dsDNA, which can integrate into the host chromosome and become a permanent part of the host" (lines 10364-10368). Because the host replicates DNA only from a DNA template, it possesses no enzyme that makes DNA from an RNA template; the drug therefore hits a step that exists only in the viral life cycle, which is exactly why reverse transcriptase inhibitors "block the early step of converting viral RNA genome into DNA" using "competitive nucleoside analog inhibitors (e.g., azidothymidine/zidovudine, or AZT)" while sparing the host (lines 26191-26192). (Choice A) Host DNA-dependent DNA polymerase is the cell's OWN replication enzyme; inhibiting it would halt the patient's chromosomal DNA replication, directly contradicting the stem's statement that host DNA continues to replicate normally, and a retrovirus does not use the host polymerase to copy its RNA genome. (Choice B) A viral DNA-dependent DNA polymerase copies a DNA template; some DNA viruses (e.g., herpesviruses, the target of acyclovir's "viral DNA polymerase") carry such an enzyme, but a retrovirus must first copy an RNA template, a job a DNA-dependent polymerase cannot do, so this is the wrong enzyme class for this virus. (Choice C) Correct: reverse transcriptase is an RNA-dependent DNA polymerase unique to the viral life cycle, making it a selective target that spares host DNA replication. (Choice D) Integrase acts later, splicing the already-synthesized DNA copy into the host chromosome; it does not perform the RNA-to-DNA conversion the stem describes, which is the step the drug blocks. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must combine the retroviral replication mechanism with the principle of selective toxicity to deduce which enzyme is both responsible for RNA-to-DNA conversion and absent from the host.$ex94$,
    'hard', '2B', $cc94$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc94$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a94A$Targets the host's own DNA polymerase, ignoring that this would block host replication too (selective-toxicity reversed)$a94A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', $a94B$510-tempting DNA-virus polymerase trap: correct that it is viral and host lacks it, but it copies DNA not RNA (acyclovir/herpes confusion)$a94B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a94D$Names a real later-step viral enzyme (integrase) and mis-assigns the RNA-to-DNA conversion to it$a94D$ FROM q;

-- Q18 [Viral Life Cycle: Lytic and Lysogenic · medium · Skill 2 · ans D] (g95)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Viral Life Cycle: Lytic and Lysogenic',
    $qt95$A virulent T4-type bacteriophage infects an E. coli cell. A researcher records five events but logs them out of order: host lysis, injection of the phage genome, self-assembly of new phage particles, adsorption to a surface receptor, and hijacking of host machinery to copy viral components. Which sequence places these events in the order they actually occur during the lytic cycle?$qt95$,
    $op95$[{"label":"A","text":"Injection of the phage genome \\(\\rightarrow\\) adsorption to a surface receptor \\(\\rightarrow\\) hijacking of host machinery \\(\\rightarrow\\) self-assembly \\(\\rightarrow\\) host lysis"},{"label":"B","text":"Adsorption to a surface receptor \\(\\rightarrow\\) injection of the phage genome \\(\\rightarrow\\) self-assembly \\(\\rightarrow\\) hijacking of host machinery \\(\\rightarrow\\) host lysis"},{"label":"C","text":"Adsorption to a surface receptor \\(\\rightarrow\\) hijacking of host machinery \\(\\rightarrow\\) injection of the phage genome \\(\\rightarrow\\) self-assembly \\(\\rightarrow\\) host lysis"},{"label":"D","text":"Adsorption to a surface receptor \\(\\rightarrow\\) injection of the phage genome \\(\\rightarrow\\) hijacking of host machinery \\(\\rightarrow\\) self-assembly \\(\\rightarrow\\) host lysis"}]$op95$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex95$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is D because the lytic cycle proceeds in a fixed order: the phage must first attach to a surface receptor, then inject its genome, then commandeer the host to make viral components, then assemble those components into new particles, and only then lyse the cell to release progeny. OpenStax Microbiology lists "five stages in the bacteriophage lytic cycle": attachment ("the phage interacts with specific bacterial surface receptors"), entry or penetration ("contraction of the tail sheath...inject the viral genome"), biosynthesis ("it then hijacks the host cell to replicate, transcribe, and translate the necessary viral components"), maturation ("new virions are created"), and release ("Mature viruses burst out of the host cell in a process called lysis"). Each step depends on the previous one—no genome can be copied before it is injected, and no particle can self-assemble before its components are biosynthesized—so adsorption, injection, hijacking, self-assembly, and lysis is the only internally consistent order. (Choice A) Injection cannot precede adsorption, because the phage must recognize and bind a surface receptor before its tail sheath can inject the genome. (Choice B) This reverses biosynthesis and self-assembly; new particles cannot self-assemble before the host machinery is hijacked to copy the viral components that make them up. (Choice C) This places hijacking of host machinery before the genome is even injected, but the phage genome must enter the cell before it can direct the host to copy viral components. (Choice D) This is the order observed: adsorption, injection (penetration), biosynthesis (hijacking), maturation (self-assembly), and release (lysis). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the dependency relationships among the lytic-cycle stages to reconstruct their correct temporal sequence from a scrambled list.$ex95$,
    'medium', '2B', $cc95$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc95$, 'biology', 2, 100
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a95A$swaps the first two steps so genome injection precedes receptor attachment$a95A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a95B$transposes biosynthesis and maturation so particles assemble before components are made$a95B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a95C$places host-machinery takeover before the genome is injected into the cell$a95C$ FROM q;

-- Q19 [Viral Life Cycle: Lytic and Lysogenic · hard · Skill 2 · ans A] (g96)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Viral Life Cycle: Lytic and Lysogenic',
    $qt96$A researcher maintains a culture of E. coli carrying a lambda-type bacteriophage whose DNA has integrated into the bacterial chromosome and is being copied along with the chromosome at each cell division, with no phage particles produced. After the culture is briefly exposed to UV radiation that damages bacterial DNA, the cells abruptly begin lysing and releasing new phages. Which event MOST directly accounts for the sudden burst of phage production?$qt96$,
    $op96$[{"label":"A","text":"The UV-induced DNA damage triggered induction, prompting the prophage to excise from the host chromosome and enter the lytic cycle."},{"label":"B","text":"The UV radiation repaired the integrated phage DNA, stabilizing it as a prophage and driving the cells deeper into the lysogenic state."},{"label":"C","text":"The UV exposure caused the bacteria to take up free phage DNA released by neighboring cells, initiating infection through transformation."},{"label":"D","text":"The UV radiation mutated the origin of replication so the prophage could no longer be copied with the chromosome and was diluted out as the cells divided."}]$op96$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex96$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is A because a phage whose genome is integrated into the host chromosome and copied silently with each division is a prophage in the lysogenic cycle, and DNA-damaging stress such as UV is the classic trigger that induces it to excise and switch to the lytic cycle. The text states that during the lysogenic cycle the phage genome "integrates into the bacterial chromosome and becomes part of the host," that the "integrated phage genome is called a prophage," and that "as the bacterium replicates its chromosome, it also replicates the phage's DNA and passes it on to new daughter cells" (lines 10243-10248) — exactly the silent, division-coupled copying described in the scenario. The text further explains that "some conditions (e.g., ultraviolet light exposure or chemical exposure) stimulate the prophage to undergo induction, causing the phage to excise from the genome, enter the lytic cycle, and produce new phages to leave host cells" (lines 10287-10289), and Figure 6.8 notes that "environmental stressors such as starvation or exposure to toxic chemicals may cause the prophage to be excised and enter the lytic cycle" (lines 10263-10264). The sudden lysis and phage release therefore reflect UV-driven induction of the prophage. (Choice A) Correct: UV-induced DNA damage causes induction, excision of the prophage, and entry into the lytic cycle, producing the burst of phage and lysis described. (Choice B) Reversed: UV does not repair phage DNA or deepen latency; DNA damage is precisely the stress that ends latency and pushes the prophage out of the lysogenic state into lysis. (Choice C) Wrong mechanism: this describes transformation, the uptake of naked DNA from the environment, which is a horizontal gene transfer process, not the activation of an already-integrated prophage; the new phages here come from the resident provirus, not from imported DNA. (Choice D) Wrong mechanism: prophages are not lost by passive dilution during division — the scenario shows active lysis and phage release, which is induction, not failure to replicate; the text describes induction and excision, not loss of the replication origin. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the clues in the scenario (integrated genome, silent copying with division, abrupt lysis after UV) to infer that the virus is in the lysogenic state and predict that UV-induced DNA damage is the stressor that triggers the switch to the lytic cycle.$ex96$,
    'hard', '2B', $cc96$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc96$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a96B$Treats the DNA-damaging stressor as a stabilizing/repair signal that reinforces latency, the exact reverse of induction.$a96B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a96C$Substitutes transformation (uptake of environmental naked DNA) for induction of an already-integrated prophage.$a96C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a96D$Assumes prophages are passively diluted out rather than actively induced and excised, ignoring the observed lysis and phage release.$a96D$ FROM q;

-- Q20 [Viral Life Cycle: Lytic and Lysogenic · hard · Skill 2 · ans B] (g97)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Viral Life Cycle: Lytic and Lysogenic',
    $qt97$An enveloped RNA virus replicating in a cultured host cell continuously sheds new virions, each acquiring its envelope from a patch of the plasma membrane as it pushes outward through the intact membrane. The host cell remains alive and keeps producing virus for an extended period. Which explanation BEST accounts for why this release mode allows progeny to be released continuously without killing the host?$qt97$,
    $op97$[{"label":"A","text":"Each budding event tears the membrane slightly, and the accumulated damage causes the cell to lyse and liberate its progeny in a single burst, so budding is simply a slower route to lysis."},{"label":"B","text":"As each virion buds outward it is wrapped by a patch of plasma membrane rather than rupturing the cell all at once, so the membrane stays intact and the cell can keep assembling and releasing virions over a prolonged, productive infection."},{"label":"C","text":"The virus must have integrated its genome into the host chromosome as a provirus and gone dormant, so no new virions are produced until the provirus is later induced."},{"label":"D","text":"Budding removes large patches of plasma membrane that cannot reseal, so the cell loses cytoplasm osmotically and dies within a single replication cycle."}]$op97$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex97$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is B because an enveloped virus that exits by budding takes a small patch of the host's phospholipid membrane as its envelope without rupturing the cell, so the host membrane remains intact and the cell can continue assembling and releasing progeny over time as a productive infection that does not kill it. The text states that an enveloped virion's envelope "is a small portion of phospholipid membrane obtained as the virion buds from a host cell," and that new virions "are released by budding" (lines 9973-9974, 10333-10334). Because release by budding does not disrupt the cell wall/membrane the way lysis does, the virus "may remain silent or undergo productive infection without seriously harming or killing the host," which the text identifies as a persistent (chronic) infection (lines 10395-10396). This contrasts with the lytic cycle, in which "mature viruses burst out of the host cell in a process called lysis," killing it (line 10238). The reasoning chains two steps: (1) budding acquires membrane piecemeal rather than rupturing the cell, and (2) an intact cell can keep producing virus, yielding continuous release without host death. (Choice A) is a partial-truth/lytic-reversal trap: it correctly notes budding involves the membrane but wrongly converts budding into delayed lysis with a single burst, which describes the lytic cycle, not budding-based release. (Choice B) is correct because budding wraps each virion in membrane without lysing the cell, permitting prolonged productive release. (Choice C) confuses budding with latency/integration; a provirus that has gone dormant produces no new virions, contradicting the stem's statement that the cell is continuously shedding virus. (Choice D) is a misconception: budding removes only a small patch of membrane that the cell can replace, not a destructive loss that causes osmotic death within one cycle. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of envelope acquisition by budding to predict why this exit route, unlike lysis, leaves the host cell intact and able to sustain continuous virion production.$ex97$,
    'hard', '2B', $cc97$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc97$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'partial_truth', $a97A$Budding reframed as delayed lysis (lytic-reversal trap): correctly invokes the membrane but converts continuous budding into a single lytic burst$a97A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a97C$Confuses budding-based productive release with proviral integration/latency, in which no virions are made until induction$a97C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a97D$Treats budding as osmotically destructive membrane loss that kills the cell within one cycle$a97D$ FROM q;

-- Q21 [Viral Life Cycle: Lytic and Lysogenic · medium · Skill 2 · ans C] (g99)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Viral Life Cycle: Lytic and Lysogenic',
    $qt99$A temperate bacteriophage integrates its genome into the host bacterial chromosome, where the viral DNA is then replicated passively along with the chromosome through many bacterial generations, yielding no progeny virions until an environmental stressor later triggers a switch in its behavior. This integrated, dormant state corresponds to which life cycle, and what is the integrated viral genome termed in this state?$qt99$,
    $op99$[{"label":"A","text":"The lytic cycle; the integrated genome is a virion"},{"label":"B","text":"Transduction; the integrated genome is a plasmid"},{"label":"C","text":"The lysogenic cycle; the integrated genome is a prophage"},{"label":"D","text":"The productive cycle; the integrated genome is a capsomere"}]$op99$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex99$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is C because the integrated, dormant state in which a temperate phage genome is incorporated into the bacterial chromosome and copied passively with it is the lysogenic cycle, and the integrated genome in that state is specifically called a prophage. The text states that \"During the lysogenic cycle, instead of killing the host, the phage genome integrates into the bacterial chromosome and becomes part of the host. The integrated phage genome is called a prophage\" (lines 10243-10245). It further notes that \"As the bacterium replicates its chromosome, it also replicates the phage's DNA and passes it on to new daughter cells during reproduction\" (line 10247), matching the passive replication across generations, and that the prophage persists \"until induction\" (line 10254) by an environmental signal. (Choice A) is wrong because the lytic cycle is the active route in which \"the bacteriophage takes over the cell, reproduces new phages, and destroys the cell\" (lines 10210-10211), and a virion is a \"new virus particle\" (line 9803), not an integrated genome. (Choice B) is wrong because transduction is a gene-transfer event in which \"a bacteriophage transfers bacterial DNA from one bacterium to another\" (line 10277), not a viral life cycle, and a plasmid is a distinct DNA element, not the integrated phage genome. (Choice C) is correct, naming both the lysogenic cycle and the prophage. (Choice D) is wrong because there is no \"productive cycle\" describing this integrated state, and a capsomere is a structural \"protein subunit\" of the capsid (lines 9961-9962), not a genome. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must map a described molecular scenario (integration plus passive replication plus inducible dormancy) onto the correct named cycle and the correct name for the integrated genome.$ex99$,
    'medium', '2B', $cc99$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc99$, 'biology', 2, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a99A$Pairs the opposing active route (lytic cycle) with the free particle term (virion), conflating release with integration$a99A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a99B$Substitutes a horizontal gene-transfer process (transduction) and a different DNA element (plasmid) for the dormant cycle and its integrated genome$a99B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a99D$Invents a release-mode label (productive cycle) and names a capsid subunit (capsomere) as if it were the integrated genome$a99D$ FROM q;

-- Q22 [Virus Structure · easy · Skill 1 · ans D] (g87)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Virus Structure',
    $qt87$An electron micrograph of an isolated virion reveals a single molecule of nucleic acid packed inside a geometric shell that is built entirely from many copies of repeating protein subunits, with no lipid present. The protein shell directly enclosing the genome is the:$qt87$,
    $op87$[{"label":"A","text":"envelope"},{"label":"B","text":"spike"},{"label":"C","text":"capsomere"},{"label":"D","text":"capsid"}]$op87$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex87$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is D because the protein coat that surrounds and directly encloses a virus's nucleic acid genome is, by definition, the capsid. The text states that viruses "consist of a nucleic acid (either RNA or DNA, but never both) surrounded by a protein coat called a capsid," and that "each capsid is composed of protein subunits called capsomeres made of one or more different types of capsomere proteins that interlock to form the closely packed capsid." The micrograph shows exactly this arrangement: one nucleic acid molecule inside a geometric protein shell assembled from repeating subunits, with no lipid, which is the naked (nonenveloped) capsid. (Choice A) The envelope is incorrect because it is "a small portion of phospholipid membrane obtained as the virion buds from a host cell," a lipid bilayer outside the capsid — and the micrograph explicitly shows no lipid. (Choice B) A spike is incorrect because spikes are "protein structures" that extend "outward and away from the capsid" to "attach and enter a cell"; they are surface projections, not the shell enclosing the genome. (Choice C) A capsomere is incorrect because it names the individual "protein subunits" that "interlock to form the closely packed capsid" — a building block of the shell, not the whole shell that encloses the genome. (Choice D) The capsid is correct because it is the protein coat that surrounds the viral nucleic acid. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the protein shell enclosing a viral genome is called the capsid.$ex87$,
    'easy', '2B', $cc87$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc87$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a87A$Confusing the outer lipid envelope (a phospholipid layer, present only in enveloped viruses) with the protein coat that encloses the genome$a87A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a87B$Naming a real protein viral structure (attachment spike) that is also made of protein but projects from the surface rather than enclosing the genome$a87B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a87C$Substituting the individual protein subunit (capsomere) for the assembled whole shell (capsid) it builds$a87C$ FROM q;

-- Q23 [Virus Structure · easy · Skill 1 · ans A] (g88)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Virus Structure',
    $qt88$A virologist purifies intact virions and assays them for the molecular machinery of protein synthesis, finding none of it present. This absence is precisely why the virus CANNOT make its own proteins and must instead commandeer a host cell. Which structure, normally responsible for translating mRNA into polypeptides, is the virus missing?$qt88$,
    $op88$[{"label":"A","text":"Ribosomes"},{"label":"B","text":"A protein capsid"},{"label":"C","text":"A nucleic acid genome"},{"label":"D","text":"A phospholipid envelope"}]$op88$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex88$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is A because ribosomes are the structures that translate mRNA into polypeptides, and viruses do not possess them, which is exactly why a virus must hijack a host cell's machinery to synthesize its proteins. Viruses are noncellular and therefore "lack most of the components of cells, such as organelles, ribosomes, and the plasma membrane" (OpenStax Biology 2e, line 24563). Because they have no ribosomes of their own, viruses "must invade a living cell, which serves as a host, and then take over the host's cellular apparatus" to reproduce (OpenStax Biology 2e, lines 11380-11381). A virion is built from a nucleic acid genome and a capsid (and sometimes an envelope), but none of these can perform translation, so the assay correctly finds no protein-synthesis machinery in the purified particle. (Choice A) is correct: ribosomes are the translation machinery, and their absence forces the virus to use host ribosomes to make viral proteins. (Choice B) is incorrect: the protein capsid is a structure viruses DO have ("a nucleic acid core, an outer protein coating or capsid," line 24564); it packages the genome but does not translate mRNA. (Choice C) is incorrect: a nucleic acid genome (DNA or RNA, but never both) is present in every virion and is the template for proteins, not the machinery that builds them. (Choice D) is incorrect: a phospholipid envelope is found only on enveloped viruses ("an outer envelope made of protein and phospholipid membranes derived from the host cell," lines 24564-24565) and is involved in entry, not protein synthesis. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that ribosomes are the organelle of translation and that viruses, being acellular, lack them.$ex88$,
    'easy', '2B', $cc88$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc88$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
