-- Q24 [Virus Structure · medium · Skill 2 · ans B] (g89)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Virus Structure',
    $qt89$A virologist purifies two viruses: one whose nucleic-acid–packed capsid is wrapped in a host-derived phospholipid envelope studded with attachment glycoproteins, and one that is a bare protein capsid with no surrounding membrane. Each preparation is treated with a lipid-dissolving detergent and then mixed with cultured host cells. Which outcome is the virologist MOST likely to observe?$qt89$,
    $op89$[{"label":"A","text":"Both viruses lose infectivity, because the detergent disrupts the protein capsid that each virus depends on for attachment."},{"label":"B","text":"The enveloped virus loses infectivity while the naked virus remains infectious, because the detergent strips the phospholipid envelope and its attachment glycoproteins."},{"label":"C","text":"The naked virus loses infectivity while the enveloped virus remains infectious, because only the naked virus relies on lipid for host attachment."},{"label":"D","text":"Both viruses remain infectious, because a virus carries the enzymes needed to rebuild any membrane the detergent removes."}]$op89$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex89$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is B because the lipid-dissolving detergent destroys the phospholipid envelope (and the attachment glycoproteins embedded in it) that the enveloped virus needs to bind host cells, whereas the naked virus has only a protein capsid, which lipid solvents do not dissolve. OpenStax distinguishes the two virion types by their outer layer: "Viruses formed from only a nucleic acid and capsid are called naked viruses or nonenveloped viruses. Viruses formed with a nucleic-acid packed capsid surrounded by a lipid layer are called enveloped viruses" (line 9964-9973), and notes that "The viral envelope is a small portion of phospholipid membrane obtained as the virion buds from a host cell" (line 9973-9974). Because the enveloped virus carries its attachment spikes "embedded in its envelope" while a naked virus uses "spikes made of glycoproteins from its capsid" (line 9988-9990), dissolving the lipid layer disables only the enveloped virus's ability to attach and enter, sparing the detergent-resistant protein capsid of the naked virus. (Choice A) is wrong because detergents dissolve lipid, not the protein capsid, so the naked virus's capsid stays intact and infectious. (Choice B) is correct because only the enveloped virion presents a lipid layer (with its glycoprotein spikes) that the detergent can strip, abolishing attachment. (Choice C) reverses the relationship — it is the enveloped virus, not the naked one, that depends on a lipid layer. (Choice D) is wrong because viruses lack the ribosomes and biosynthetic machinery to manufacture membrane on their own; an envelope is acquired only by budding from a host cell. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the structural difference between enveloped and naked virions to predict the differential effect of a lipid solvent on infectivity.$ex89$,
    'medium', '2B', $cc89$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc89$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
