-- Q25 [Virus Structure · hard · Skill 2 · ans C] (g90)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Virus Structure',
    $qt90$A bacteriophage is engineered so that its tail fibers are replaced with the tail fibers from a different phage that naturally infects a separate bacterial species; the engineered phage's polyhedral head, tail sheath, and packaged genome are all left unchanged. A researcher then exposes a mixed culture containing both the phage's original host species and the donor phage's host species to the engineered particle. Which outcome is MOST likely?$qt90$,
    $op90$[{"label":"A","text":"The engineered phage now adsorbs to the donor's host species and injects the donor phage's genome, producing donor-type progeny inside that species."},{"label":"B","text":"The engineered phage attaches to and lyses both host species equally well, because retaining its original sheath preserves its ability to penetrate its original host."},{"label":"C","text":"The engineered phage now attaches to and infects the donor's host species rather than its original host, but the progeny it generates carry its own unchanged genome."},{"label":"D","text":"The engineered phage attaches to the donor's host species but CANNOT inject, because the swapped tail fibers are the structure that drives genome penetration through the cell wall."}]$op90$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex90$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is C because the tail fibers determine which surface receptors the phage recognizes, so swapping them redirects attachment to a new host species, but the genome the particle injects and the progeny it builds are dictated by the unchanged packaged genome. In a complex bacteriophage, \"the sheath connects the head to the tail fibers and tail pins that help the virus attach to receptors on the host cell's surface,\" and attachment is \"the first stage in the infection process in which the phage interacts with specific bacterial surface receptors,\" with \"most phages\" having \"a narrow host range\" set by this \"unique recognition.\" Penetration is a separate step performed by \"contraction of the tail sheath, which acts like a hypodermic needle to inject the viral genome,\" and the genome injected is the one packaged in the head. Therefore the new fibers grant recognition of the donor's host, the original host is no longer recognized, and the offspring are encoded by the resident genome, not the donor's. (Choice A) reverses the logic: tail fibers control which receptor is bound, not which genome is delivered, so the particle still injects and replicates its own unchanged genome, not the donor phage's. (Choice B) is wrong because host range is set by the fibers' receptor specificity, not the sheath; losing the original fibers removes recognition of the original host, so it does not infect both species. (Choice C) is correct because receptor recognition (and thus host range) tracks the tail fibers while genome identity tracks the unchanged head contents. (Choice D) confuses two steps: attachment depends on the tail fibers, but penetration is driven by sheath contraction injecting the genome, so swapped fibers that successfully attach do not block injection. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must combine the structural role of tail fibers in receptor-specific attachment with the separate roles of the sheath in penetration and the head in genome identity to predict how a targeted structural swap reshapes infection behavior.$ex90$,
    'hard', '2B', $cc90$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc90$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
